  A hash table stores key-value pairs so that a lookup takes, on average, constant time. That beats a sorted list or a
  balanced tree, whose lookups slow as the data grows, and a plain list, which must be scanned from the start. The
  trick is to turn a key into an array position, so the program jumps straight to the data instead of searching for it.
  Most languages build their dictionaries and sets on this idea.

  The conversion is done by a [0m[1mhash function[0m, which maps a key of any size to an integer. A good one is fast, gives the
  same output for the same input, and spreads keys evenly, so that similar keys such as "user1" and "user2" land far
  apart. The table takes that integer modulo the array length to pick a [0m[1mbucket[0m. Cryptographic strength is not needed
  here, because speed matters more than secrecy.

  Two different keys will sometimes land in the same bucket, an event called a [0m[1mcollision[0m. Collisions cannot be avoided,
  because the number of possible keys far exceeds the number of buckets. Every table therefore needs a rule for
  handling them, and two rules dominate:

  1. [0m[1mChaining [0mkeeps a small list in each bucket, and a lookup walks that list until it finds the key.
  2. [0m[1mOpen addressing [0mkeeps every entry in the array itself, and on a collision probes other slots in a fixed sequence
     until it finds a free one.

  A minimal table with chaining fits in a few lines of Python:

